    "Linear Algebra Foundations and Decompositions": "The material covers fundamental linear algebra concepts with an emphasis on Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization, linear transforms, eigenvalue decomposition, trace, determinants, and matrix properties. Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization constructs an orthogonal basis from a set of vectors (e.g., u1 = a1, u2 = a2 - (u1^T a2)/(u1^T u1) u1), and results in a zero vector if a vector is linearly dependent or a multiple of previous vectors. Eigenvalue decompositions, symmetric matrices, and the power method form core computational techniques for analyzing matrix structure.",
    "Matrix Optimization and Gradients": "Focuses on vector- and matrix-valued function differentiation, matrix calculus, gradients of determinants/log-determinants, descent directions, and matrix square roots or decompositions (such as eigendecompositions of covariance matrices C = E Lambda E^T and their matrix square roots C^(1/2) = E diag(sqrt(lambda_1), ..., sqrt(lambda_D)) E^T). Whitening matrices and independent component analysis (ICA) models are also analyzed in the context of parameter optimization and transformation.",
    "Directed Graphical Models": "Examines directed acyclic graphs (DAGs), conditional independence structures, ordered and local Markov properties, d-separation, canonical connections, and hidden Markov models. Discusses how joint probability distributions factorize according to the DAG topology and how marginal or conditional independencies can be read off from the graph structure.",
    "Undirected Graphical Models and Factor Graphs": "Covers undirected graphical models (Markov random fields), Gibbs distributions, factorisation, Markov blankets, pairwise potentials, restricted Boltzmann machines (RBMs), and conversion of directed/undirected models into factor graphs. Factor graphs explicitly represent the factorization of a joint probability distribution into local factor nodes connected to variable nodes, such as in hidden Markov models or general conditional probability tables.",
    "Inference, Learning, and Monte Carlo Methods": "Addresses parameter estimation and probabilistic inference, including maximum likelihood estimation (MLE), Bayesian inference (e.g., for Bernoulli models and Gaussians), factor analysis, independent component analysis (ICA), score matching, Monte Carlo integration, importance sampling, inverse transform sampling, rejection sampling, Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) inference, and variational inference (such as mean-field approximations)."
}"""
