- Two vectors $a_1$ and $a_2$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$ span a parallelogram with a squared area given by $S^2 = (a_2^T a_2)(a_1^T a_1) - (a_2^T a_1)^2$.
- The area of a parallelogram equals the length of the base vector times the length of the orthogonal height vector.
- The Gram-Schmidt process can be applied to vectors $a_1$ and $a_2$ to generate an orthogonal vector $u_2$.
- Any linear combination $v = \alpha a_1 + \beta a_2$ can be expressed in terms of the orthogonal vectors $u_1$ and $u_2$."""
