  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""- **Definition/core idea**: Maximum likelihood estimation for a Gaussian involves determining the parameters—specifically the mean $\mu$ and standard deviation $\sigma$—that maximize the likelihood of observing a given set of independent and identically distributed (iid) data.
- **Key points**: 
  - The Gaussian probability density function is parametrized by $\theta = (\mu, \sigma)$.
  - For zero-mean Gaussians, the second moment $m_2$ serves as the maximum likelihood estimate of the variance.
  - Score matching and maximum likelihood estimates yield the same result for zero-mean Gaussians.
  - This equivalence between score matching and maximum likelihood estimates also holds true for multivariate Gaussian models."""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
