  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""- **Definition/core idea**: Factor analysis models visible variables using lower-dimensional latent variables through a Gaussian conditional distribution and factor loadings. 
- **Key points**:
  - The latent variables have a prior distribution defined by covariance matrix $C$ and mean zero.
  - The conditional distribution of visibles $v$ given latent $h$ is a Gaussian $N(v; Fh + c, \Psi)$, where $F$ represents the matrix of factor loadings.
  - $\Psi$ is a diagonal matrix containing specific variances, and $c$ is a vector in the visible dimension space.
  - The dimension of the latent variables $H$ is typically less than the dimension of the visible variables $D$.
- **Example**: A standard factor analysis model is a special case obtained when the latent covariance matrix $C$ is set to the identity matrix."""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
