  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""{
    "Linear Algebra Foundations and Decompositions": "Covered within Michael U. Gutmann's pen-and-paper machine learning exercises, focusing on linear algebra fundamentals. Specific topics include Gram-Schmidt orthogonalisation (proving orthogonality of generated vectors, handling linear combinations, and behavior when a vector is a linear combination of previous ones), linear transforms, eigenvalue decomposition, traces, determinants, eigenvalues, eigenvalue decomposition for symmetric matrices, and the power method.",
    "Optimization Techniques for Vector and Matrix Functions": "Addresses mathematical optimization tools used in machine learning models. Topics include gradients of vector-valued functions, Newton's method (involving Hessians and search directions), gradients of matrix-valued functions defined via derivatives or trace expansions, gradients of log-determinants, and descent directions for matrix-valued functions.",
    "Directed Graphical Models and Markov Properties": "Covers probability distributions represented over directed acyclic graphs. Subjects include directed graph concepts, canonical connections, ordered and local Markov properties, d-separation, chest clinic examples, hidden Markov models, and alternative or extended characterisations of independencies in directed graphical models.",
    "Undirected Graphical Models and Factorisation": "Examines Markov random fields and Gibbs distributions. Key concepts include visualising and analysing Gibbs distributions via undirected graphs, factorisation properties, independencies, factorisation derived from Markov blankets, undirected graphical models with pairwise potentials, restricted Boltzmann machines, and connections between hidden Markov models and changes of measure.",
    "Factor Graphs, Message Passing, and Inference": "Focuses on exact and approximate inference mechanics using factor graphs. Topics include converting directed and undirected graphical models into factor graphs, sum-product message passing algorithms, max-sum message passing, and the choice of elimination order in factor graphs for efficient computation.",
    "Model-Based Learning, Sampling, and Variational Inference": "Covers parameter estimation, sampling techniques, and variational methods. Topics include maximum likelihood estimation (e.g., for Gaussians and probability tables in fully observed directed models), Bayesian inference (for Bernoulli models and probability tables), factor analysis, independent component analysis, score matching, Monte Carlo integration, importance sampling, inverse transform sampling, rejection sampling, Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) inference, and mean field variational inference."
}"""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
