DEBUG    | 2026-09-16 17:20:04 | course_creator:run_adk_topic_pipeline:230 | Event from WebSearchAgent: model_version='gemini-3.5-flash-lite' content=Content(
  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""{
    "document_notes": "Document Section 2.4 and 2.5 cover gradients of log-determinants and descent directions for matrix-valued functions:\n- The gradient of the log-determinant (or related matrix objective) yields expressions like $\\nabla J(W) = W^{-\\top}$. Specifically, using eigenvalue decompositions or inverse matrix representations ($W^{-1} = \\sum_i \\frac{1}{\\lambda_i} u_i v_i^\\top$), the gradient can be derived.\n- Descent directions for matrix-valued functions $J(W)$ in gradient descent use the update equation $W \\leftarrow W - \\epsilon \\nabla J(W)$ with step-length $\\epsilon$.\n- First-order approximation to the perturbed objective function: $J(W - \\epsilon \\nabla J(W)) = J(W) - \\epsilon \\text{tr}(\\nabla J(W)^\\top \\nabla J(W)) + O(\\epsilon^2)$.\n- For any nonzero matrix $M$, $\\text{tr}(M^\\top M) = \\sum_{ij} (M_{ji})^2 > 0$. Therefore, $\\text{tr}(\\nabla J(W)^\\top \\nabla J(W)) > 0$ for a nonzero gradient, ensuring $J(W - \\epsilon \\nabla J(W)) < J(W)$ for small enough $\\epsilon$, making $\\nabla J(W)$ a valid descent direction.\n- More generally, transformations like $A^\\top A \\nabla J(W) B B^\\top$ also serve as descent directions (or leave the objective invariant), which can be proven by evaluating $J(W - \\epsilon A^\\top A \\nabla J(W) B B^\\top)$ and showing that the trace term reduces to $\\text{tr}(M^\\top M) \\ge 0$ where $M = A \\nabla J(W) B$.",
    "web_notes": "Web search results point to resources discussing the derivative of the log determinant (e.g., for Hermitian positive-definite matrices or multi-variable quantum algorithms for log-determinant gradients), as well as geometric applications like affine normal directions via log-determinant geometry. Specific standard mathematical formulations of the log-determinant gradient state that for a matrix $W$ (or positive definite matrix), the gradient of $\\ln \\det(W)$ is $W^{-\\top}$ (or $W^{-1}$ depending on parametrization and layout conventions).",
    "used_web": true
}"""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
