DEBUG    | 2026-09-16 17:20:06 | course_creator:run_adk_topic_pipeline:230 | Event from ContentWriterAgent: model_version='gemini-3.5-flash-lite' content=Content(
  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""- **Definition/core idea**: Minimizing matrix-valued functions using gradient descent relies on the gradient $\nabla J(W)$ defining a valid first-order descent direction to decrease the objective function value.
- **Key points**:
  - The update equation for matrix-valued gradient descent is given by $W \leftarrow W - \epsilon \nabla J(W)$ with step-length $\epsilon$.
  - A first-order Taylor approximation of the perturbed objective yields $J(W - \epsilon \nabla J(W)) = J(W) - \epsilon \text{tr}(\nabla J(W)^\top \nabla J(W)) + O(\epsilon^2)$.
  - For any nonzero matrix $M$, the trace expression satisfies $\text{tr}(M^\top M) = \sum_{ij} (M_{ji})^2 > 0$.
  - Consequently, for a nonzero gradient, $\text{tr}(\nabla J(W)^\top \nabla J(W)) > 0$, ensuring $J(W - \epsilon \nabla J(W)) < J(W)$ for small enough $\epsilon$.
  - Transformed directions such as $A^\top A \nabla J(W) B B^\top$ also act as valid descent directions or leave the objective invariant.

## From the Web
